{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 200,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A little later today (April 8th) a ball of rock the size \nof Australia will drift directly between the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5680,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth and theSun. That ball—our Moon—will \ncast a shadow that will pass across the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10560,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "North American continent, carving a narrow \nline from Mexico to Newfoundland. For anyone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16000,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lucky enough to be on that line the sky will go \ntwilight-dark for up to four and a half minutes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22360,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the Sun will be transformed into a black \ndisk crowned with the ghostly filaments of its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26960,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "corona. will witness a total solar eclipse. Of \nall the astronomical phenomena you can witness,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34960,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the total solar eclipse has to be the most \nvisceral--the most in-your-face reminder that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our reality consists of giant balls of rock \nspinning around stars. And it's also the eclipse  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46920,
    "dDurationMs": 6816,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and phenomena like it that set us on the path to \nunderstanding that reality in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59760,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On this show we tend to speak from a position \nof already knowing stuff. We know that space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65720,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is big and that moons orbit planets orbit \nstars, and we go from there to some pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crazy places. . But imagine what it must \nhave been like to know nothing. I mean,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76840,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nothing about the scale of the cosmos or the \nnature of the solar system. Imagine how terrifying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83560,
    "dDurationMs": 8600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and mysterious a total solar eclipse would be. \nBut also inspiring--you might feel inspired to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92160,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "figure out what in the world--or out of it--was \ngoing on. And in fact the eclipse and phenomena  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like it were the very first tools used by the \nancients on our path from that place of mystery  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104720,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and not-knowing to our modern understanding of \nthe universe. So, today, as a celebration of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the impending eclipse I want to retrace some of \nthat cognitive journey so that when the eclipse  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens we can appreciate a bit better how \nwe know what we know about what we're seeing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121880,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This particular telling of how we figured \nout the universe starts with the ancient  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125680,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "greeks. Many other peoples contributed \ndifferent things to our modern story,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130440,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it was the Greeks who kickstarted the whole \nscience thing. So what did they figure out? Well,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137320,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "despite the common notion that we thought \nthe world was flat until Christopher Columbus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sailed around it, the ancient Greeks were \nwell aware of the roundness of the planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148880,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They knew that boats disappear ‘hull’ first \nas they head out to sea with their large  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sails disappearing last. They knew \nthat, when moving north or sound,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160840,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the altitude of stars change in the way you’d \nexpect for moving on the surface of a sphere.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 166560,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And they knew the Earth was round because \nof eclipses. Lunar eclipses in this case.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172760,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As long as you know that these are caused \nby the Moon moving through Earth’s shadow,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s hard to imagine a non-round object \ncausing that distinctly round shadow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And, as it happens, the ancient Greeks were \nfully aware of the cause of both lunar and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188480,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar eclipses. That was first worked out \nby Anaxagoras in the fifth century BCE. He  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194360,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realized that only one configuration made \nconsistent sense of our observations. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 198680,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it to all make sense the Moon must travel around \nthe earth, must be spherical, and must be closer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to us than the sun. That gives us a changing \npartial view of the illuminated side of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208280,
    "dDurationMs": 8760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Moon—hence phases—and a lunar shadow for solar \neclipses and Earth shadow for lunar eclipses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the subtle details of the eclipses revealed so \nmuch more than their basic nature. As I mentioned,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222640,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the shape of Earth’s shadow pointed to our \nplanet’s roundness. There’s also the fact  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227000,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that eclipses don’t happen every lunar orbit, \nbut rather can only happen twice per year. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fact was a clue that Earth orbits the Sun. \nSee, there’s a slight misalignment between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238160,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Earth’s orbit around the Sun and the Moon’s \naround the Earth. That means the Moon usually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242760,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ducks under or over Earth’s shadow, and \nits own shadow usually missing the Earth also."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248960,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But twice a year the Moon’s orbital tilt \nis perpendicular to a line connecting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun and Earth. At those times the Moon has to \npass through Earth’s orbital plane while it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259320,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the Sun-Earth line On this “line of nodes” \nwe’ll typically get a solar and a lunar eclipses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266440,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and sometimes more than one depending on the \nMoon’s orbital position. This periodicity to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the eclipses tells us that the positions of \nthe Sun and Earth change in a regular manner,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276080,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we now understand this \nto be due to Earth’s orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, great, so eclipses gave us the basic shape \nof the Moon-Earth-Sun system. But they also give  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284920,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us the relative size scales for our growing \nmodel solar system. Those details are hidden  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291120,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in deeper subtleties of the eclipses, and \nit took our next ancient Greek—Aristarchus  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296840,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Samos—to figure them out in the 3rd century \nBCE. Aristarchus figured out three clever ways  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to use the shadows cast by celestial bodies to \nmap the solar system. The shadows of eclipses,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308640,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also the shadow that the Moon \ncasts on itself causing lunar phases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313120,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The amount of time the moon spent in its different \nphases tells us the relative distances between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317800,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth, Moon and Sun. You might think that \nthe moon spends equal time in its different  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322880,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "phases. For example, the time spent as a crescent \nshould be the same as the time spent gibbous. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329280,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simplicity, by “crescent” I’m going to mean \nanything less than half full—including a new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333720,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moon—that’s when the Moons is closer to the Sun. \nI’ll use gibbous for anything more than half full,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339200,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including actually full—when the Moon is \nfurther from the Sun. Okay, and confusingly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345880,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those half-full points are technically called \nquarter moons. Don’t ask me why. So surely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352560,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Moon should be in the crescent and gibbous \nphases for the same amount of time. After all,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357800,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Moon does spend the same amount of time on \neach side of its orbit, on average at least."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But, oddly, the Moon is gibbous for a \nbit longer than it’s a crescent. The reason  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for this is that the distance to the Sun also \nmatters. If the Sun were infinitely far away,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then indeed we’d expect equal time in gibbous and \ncrescent phases. We see a half moon when the Sun’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382640,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light illuminate the Moon at 90-degrees to our \nview of the moon. If the Sun was extremely far  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390040,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away compared to the size of the Moon’s orbit \nthen its rays arrive nearly parallel across  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that orbit, and so the half-moon points \nwould be on opposite sides of the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403640,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if the Sun isn’t “infinitely” far \ncompared to the Moon then its rays reach  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408480,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Moon at a different angle on either \nside of the Earth. The quarter-moon points  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412920,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end up being a little closer to the Sun, \nwhich means the crescent phase between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417080,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these points is shorter than the gibbous \nphase beyond these points. By measuring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422960,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the difference in the length of the \ngibbous versus crescent phases it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426600,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potentially possible to get the distance of \nthe Sun relative to the distance to the moon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431280,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quarter moon points are only about \n0.5 degrees off the locations expected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436480,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for an infinitely distant sun. That tells us \nthat the Sun is nearly 400 times more distant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443000,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the Moon. Aristarchus measured a \nbit high—he got 3 degrees, which told  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "him that the Sun is at 20 times the \nlunar distance. But remember that he  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453360,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was working without clocks and without \ntelescopes, so let's cut him some slack."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458440,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Aristarchus’s second calculation is simpler. It \nuses the fact that the Moon exactly blocks the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464760,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun during a total solar eclipse—as indeed many people witness today (April 8th). That told him that the ratio  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the size of the Moon to the size of the Sun has \nto be the same as the ratio of the distance to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477120,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moon to the distance to the Sun. Now that sure would be helpful if he knew either of those distances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483800,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that brings us to the third thing that \nAristarchus figured out, this time using lunar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489040,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eclipses. The Moon takes some time to pass through \nEarth’s shadow during such an eclipse. The width  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494680,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of that shadow depends on the size of the Earth, \nthe distance between the Earth and the Moon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also on the size to and distance of the Sun. \nAristarchus used simple geometry to write down  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505680,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some equations that related the ratios of sizes \nand distances. The ratio of sizes of the Earth to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512040,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both the Sun and Moon and the ratio of distances \nbetween Earth and both the Sun and Moon, all in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519560,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terms of the ratio of the width of Earth’s shadow \nto the Moon’s size—how many moons fit across that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526000,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shadow. And that last one could be measured just \nby watching a lunar eclipse. It’s 2.6 moons, FYI."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534080,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Aristachus concluded correctly that the Moon \nis approximately a third the diameter of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth. You might remember that he had \nalready gotten the ratio of Earth-Sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to Earth-Moon distances from the phases of \nthe Moon—and he got a number that was too  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "small. This error propagated to his estimate \nof the Sun’s size, and so he found a ratio  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between the Earth and Sun’s diameter that \nwas also about a factor of 20 too small."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559480,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this was nonetheless all very impressive. \nAristarchus, using little more than shadows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and geometry, was the first to seriously present \nevidence for our modern picture of the solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568440,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system. But you might have noticed that all these \ndistances and sizes are relative—for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s the size of the Moon or Sun compared \nto the size of the Earth. In order to pin  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down the actual scale of the solar system, \nwe needed just one real physical value."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584920,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This came from the chief librarian \nof the great library of Alexandria,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588920,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eratosthenes, also in the 3rd century \nBCE. Erastonthenes realized that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593920,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a curved world, the Sun could be \ndirectly overhead at noon at one latitude,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600240,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but at another latitude might be lower in \nthe sky. He had heard that at noon on the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605280,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "summer solstice the Sun illuminated the bottom \nof a deep well in the city of Syene, far to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611160,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "south of Alexandria—he concluded that it must \nbe directly overhead at that time and place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619080,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But he also knew that at \nthe same time in Alexandria,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun wasn’t quite overhead. A \nvertical pole would still cast a shadow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627840,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Again with simple geometry, and by measuring \nthe length of shadows cast on that day in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632280,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alexandria as well as the distance to Syene, \nEratosthenes was able to calculate the radius  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the Earth. And he got it right, to \nwithin less than 2% of the true value."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643640,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Adding Aristachus’s measurement for the size of \nthe Earth to Erastothenes’ measurements of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative sizes of Earth, Moon and Sun, we got \nour first real physical values for the sizes of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654560,
    "dDurationMs": 8120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these bodies. At the very least the size and \ndistance of the Moon was now pretty solid."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there was still the uncertainty in \nthe distance to the Sun. And we found new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "motivation to get that distance right when we \nfinally figured out the motion of the planets.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674280,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll breeze past Copernicus who verified the \nsuspicion of some of the ancient Greeks that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679600,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun really is in the center of the solar \nsystem, and go straight to Johanne Kepler.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684720,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His laws of planetary motion told us that the \nrelative speed of planets through space depends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690760,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on their distance from the Sun. Finding the \ndistance between the Earth and Sun—what we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696080,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call the Astronomical Unit—would lock into place \nour first ever precise model of the solar system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And again, it was an eclipse of sorts \nthat gave us that number. It was the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708200,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observation of another world passing \nin front of the Sun—in this case a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712400,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "planet rather than the moon, so not \nreally an eclipse, but a transit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716920,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you look at the Sun through a pinhole \ncamera when Mercury or Venus transit the Sun,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721760,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll see a tiny black dot moving slowly \nacross a bright disk. The line traveled by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the planet depends on the relative positions of \nall three bodies—but also on your own position  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733320,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the Earth. A transit observed from New \nYork might cross near the edge of that disk,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740600,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but might cross deep into the disk if observed \nin Puerto Rico. This is due to parallax—the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747200,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same effect as when your finger appears to \nmove side to side relative to the background  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752160,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when you blink your eyes back and forth. \nBut in this case it’s called solar parallax."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Measuring the difference in position of \nthe transit at two different locations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761920,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tells you the distance to the \ntransiting planet and to the Sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 766040,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There was an early attempt to measure a transit \nof Mercury back in the 1660s—but Mercury’s tiny  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size made the observation difficult and this \neffort failed. A century later the astronomer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Edmund Halley realized that Venus- being closer \nto the earth, would make the measurement easier.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only problem was that it transited much less \noften - only twice per century, eight years apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788760,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, when the regularly scheduled transits \nof Venus arrived in 1761 and 1769,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793960,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the astronomy world was prepared, scattering \nthemselves across the world to observe the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 798320,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "1769 transit from as many places as possible. \nObservations were made everywhere from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Philadelphia to St Petersburg to Tahiti that, \nwhen combined, provided the first accurate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurement of the size of the solar system. It’s \na really cool moment in the history of science- a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814880,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "massive international collaboration, requiring \nlarge government grants to fund expeditions to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the far side of the planet. It’s almost \nlike a precursor to modern ‘big science.’"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825080,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So did they succeed? Yes they did. Put all of their measurements together, and you get the distance to the sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832040,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as 153 million km. That’s only 2% above \nthe modern accepted value. With the earth’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 839360,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orbital radius and orbital period known, and \nwith the help of Kepler’s laws, the orbital  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844000,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radii of every planet in the solar system could \nbe calculated immediately. Not bad for watching  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850440,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a dot move across the face of the Sun. But this \nachievement was really the crystallization of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "two thousand years of astronomy, a final \nsynthesis of the revelations brought by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 862520,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "asymmetric lunar phases and of measuring ancient \nlunar and solar eclipses. Ultimately, though,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868480,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the credit goes to those clever humans who \nwitnessed these mystical-seeming phenomena and,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874160,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather than credit gods and myths, resolved to \nmake sense of our once-mysterious space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881600,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everybody. I wanted to let you know that \nthis episode has been a part of PBS’s Earth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887480,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Month! All this month PBS is dropping new \nepisodes celebrating the past, present,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even possible futures of our amazing planet. \nThere’s going to be lots of great science content  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "about our home base, so click the link in \nthe description to check out the full (and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902280,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "growing) playlist. And if you enjoy something, \ntell them politely that Space Time sent you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908080,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone, before we go we wanted to say thank you for all the amazing support for our Solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913080,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eclipse merch! We’re incredibly glad you \nliked it. If you haven’t done so already,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "April 9th will be the last day to buy a \nUV solar eclipse t-shirt or sweatshirt  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924000,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while supplies last. But don't worry, we’ll be \nlaunching some new merch soon. And as always,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you’d like to join us on Patreon at any level \nyou’ll be get 10% off all merch store purchases  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as well as access to the Space Time discord. And \nif you haven’t done so already, subscribing and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940960,
    "dDurationMs": 5332,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hitting the bell icon for notifications is another \ngreat way to support the Space Time community."
    } ]
  } ]
}
